1. Create a new PHP file in the `wp-content/plugins` directory of your WordPress installation. For example, `custom-block.php`.
2. In this file, add the following code to define the block:
```php
<?php
/*
Plugin Name: Custom Block
Description: A custom block with a heading, paragraph, and image.
*/

function register_custom_block() {
  wp_register_script(
    'custom-block-script',
    plugins_url( 'build/index.js', __FILE__ ),
    array( 'wp-blocks', 'wp-element', 'wp-editor' )
  );

  wp_register_style(
    'custom-block-style',
    plugins_url( 'build/style.css', __FILE__ )
  );

  register_block_type( 'custom-block/block' );
}

add_action( 'init', 'register_custom_block' );
```
This code registers a new JavaScript file and a new CSS file for the block, and registers the block type.

3. Create a new JavaScript file in the `wp-content/plugins` directory, for example `build/index.js`. This file will contain the code for the block's functionality.
```javascript
import { registerBlockType } from '@wordpress/blocks';
import { InspectorControls } from '@wordpress/block-editor';

registerBlockType( 'custom-block/block', {
  title: 'Custom Block',
  icon: 'admin-users',
  category: 'common',
  edit: Edit,
  save: Save,
} );

function Edit( { attributes, setAttributes } ) {
  const { heading, paragraph, image } = attributes;

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{ heading }</h2>
      <p>{ paragraph }</p>
      <img src={ image } />
    </div>
  );
}

function Save( { attributes } ) {
  const { heading, paragraph, image } = attributes;

  return (
    <div>
      <h2>{ heading }</h2>
      <p>{ paragraph }</p>
      <img src={ image } />
    </div>
  );
}
```
This code defines the block's title, icon, and category, and registers the block's edit and save functions.

4. Create a new CSS file in the `wp-content/plugins` directory, for example `build/style.css`. This file will contain the CSS styles for the block.
